{
  "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/10796079/",
  "title": "Abdominal decompression for suspected fetal compromise/pre-eclampsia - PubMed",
  "content": "Title/Citation\n- Abdominal decompression for suspected fetal compromise/pre-eclampsia. GJ Hofmeyr. Cochrane Database Syst Rev. 2000. Updated: Hofmeyr GJ. Cochrane Database Syst Rev. 2012 Jun 13;2012(6):CD000004. doi: 10.1002/14651858.CD000004.pub2. PMID: 22696313. Full text: https://doi.org/10.1002/14651858.CD000004\n\nObjective\n- Assess effects of antenatal abdominal decompression for maternal hypertension and/or impaired fetal growth on perinatal outcome.\n\nMethods\n- Searches: Cochrane Pregnancy and Childbirth Group trials register and Cochrane Controlled Trials Register; last search Feb 1998.\n- Inclusion: randomised or quasi-randomised trials comparing abdominal decompression versus no decompression in women with pre-eclampsia and/or suspected fetal compromise.\n- Eligibility and trial quality assessed by one reviewer.\n\nIncluded studies and quality\n- Three studies included.\n- All studies had the possibility of serious bias (methodological limitations).\n\nMain results (therapeutic abdominal decompression vs no decompression)\n- Persistent pre-eclampsia: relative risk (RR) 0.36; 95% CI 0.18 to 0.72.\n- Fetal distress in labour: RR 0.37; 95% CI 0.19 to 0.71.\n- Low birthweight: RR 0.50; 95% CI 0.40 to 0.63.\n- Apgar <6 at 1 minute: RR 0.26; 95% CI 0.12 to 0.56.\n- Perinatal mortality: RR 0.39; 95% CI 0.22 to 0.71.\n\nReviewer's conclusions\n- Effects are not clear because of methodological limitations and risk of bias in included trials.\n- Apparent improvements in birthweight and perinatal mortality justify further evaluation of abdominal decompression for impaired fetal growth and possibly for women with pre-eclampsia.\n\nOther\n- Original review date: 2000; updated review published 13 June 2012 (see citation above).",
